SA=" (Select all that apply.)"
C = [
# ===== Auscultation areas
("Where to listen","An aortic ejection click from aortic stenosis is heard BEST at which location?",["Erb point (left sternal border, 2nd–3rd intercostal space)"],["Apex","Lower left sternal border","Left infraclavicular area"],
 "The aortic ejection click is constant in intensity and best heard at Erb point. Erb point also picks up AS and a dilated aortic root.","\"Erb = Ejection click of the Aorta.\""),
("Where to listen","Which conditions are heard at the UPPER LEFT sternal border (ULSB)?"+SA,["Pulmonic valve click of pulmonary stenosis","Atrial septal defect","Patent ductus arteriosus","Pulmonary flow murmur"],["Ventricular septal defect","Mitral regurgitation"],
 "ULSB = pulmonic area: PS clicks, pulmonary flow murmurs, ASD, PDA, and venous hum. VSD is at the LLSB; mitral regurgitation is at the apex.","ULSB = the \"P-A-P\" corner: Pulmonic stenosis, ASD, PDA."),
("Where to listen","Which conditions are heard best at the LOWER LEFT sternal border (LLSB)?"+SA,["Ventricular septal defect","Still murmur","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Tricuspid regurgitation","Subaortic stenosis"],["Venous hum","Aortic valve click of aortic stenosis"],
 "LLSB: VSD, Still murmur, tricuspid regurgitation, HCM, and subaortic stenosis. Venous hum is in the upper chest; the AS valve click is at the URSB.","LLSB = \"V-STiHS\": VSD, Still, Tricuspid regurg, HCM, Subaortic."),
("Where to listen","A murmur of mitral valve regurgitation is heard best at the:",["Apex"],["Upper right sternal border","Upper left sternal border","Erb point"],
 "The apex is the mitral area: mitral regurgitation and mitral/aortic clicks.","\"Mitral = the tip (apex) of the heart.\""),
("Where to listen","Which findings belong to the UPPER RIGHT sternal border (URSB)?"+SA,["Aortic valve click of aortic stenosis","Venous hum"],["Ventricular septal defect","Mitral valve prolapse click","Still murmur"],
 "URSB = aortic area: AS valve clicks and venous hum (venous hum is also heard at the ULSB).","\"Right upper = the Aorta's door.\""),
("Where to listen","A 12-year-old's harsh grade III–IV/VI crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur is loudest at the URSB and radiates to the neck. The most likely lesion is:",["Aortic stenosis"],["Pulmonic stenosis","Ventricular septal defect","Still murmur"],
 "AS murmur: harsh, URSB, radiates to the neck. A thrill may be felt at the suprasternal notch.","\"AS goes up and to the right, then into the neck.\""),
("Where to listen","Pulmonic stenosis's systolic ejection murmur classically radiates to:",["The back and both lung fields"],["The neck only","The right axilla only","Nowhere; it never radiates"],
 "PS: harsh mid-to-late SEM at the ULSB, radiating along the LSB, to the neck, back, and both lung fields.","\"PS spreads to the Pulmonary fields (lungs and back).\""),
("Where to listen","Coarctation of the aorta's systolic ejection murmur is best heard in the:",["Left infraclavicular region, radiating to the back"],["Apex, radiating to the axilla","URSB, radiating to the neck","LLSB without radiation"],
 "The CoA murmur is heard below the left clavicle, with transmission to the back (where the narrowed aorta is).","\"Coarct = Collarbone (left) to the back.\""),
# ===== S1 S2
("Heart sounds","Which statements about S1 are correct?"+SA,["It is usually loudest at the apex","It is synchronous with the apical and carotid pulses","It is the \"lubb\" of lubb-dupp"],["It is best assessed at the upper left sternal border","It is made by aortic and pulmonic valve closure"],
 "S1 = mitral/tricuspid closure, loudest at the apex, in time with the apical/carotid pulse. S2 is aortic + pulmonic closure and is best assessed at the ULSB.","\"S1 = the Start (lubb), at the tip (apex).\""),
("Heart sounds","In a healthy child, how does S2 behave with breathing?",["It splits with inspiration and becomes single with expiration"],["It is widely split and fixed","It is always single","It splits only with expiration"],
 "On inspiration, more blood returns to the right side, so P2 lags behind A2 and S2 splits. On expiration it becomes single.","\"Breathe IN → S2 splits IN two.\""),
("Heart sounds","Where is S2 best assessed?",["Upper left sternal border (pulmonic area)"],["Apex","Lower left sternal border","Upper right sternal border"],
 "S2 splitting is one of the most important parts of the cardiac exam and is best heard in the pulmonic area (ULSB).","\"S2 → the Second rib space, left.\""),
("Heart sounds","A child has a LOUD, single S2. Which conditions could cause this?"+SA,["Pulmonary hypertension","Pulmonary atresia","Transposition of the great arteries"],["Atrial septal defect","Innocent Still murmur"],
 "Pulmonary HTN closes P2 early and accentuates S2. A missing semilunar valve (pulmonary atresia) gives a single S2. In d-TGA, S2 is loud and single because of where the great arteries sit. ASD gives a WIDE FIXED split.","\"Loud and Lonely S2 = pressure (pulm HTN) or a missing/moved valve.\""),
("Heart sounds","A wide split S2 that does NOT become single on expiration suggests:",["Increased pulmonary blood flow, as in an atrial septal defect"],["Pulmonary hypertension","Aortic stenosis","A normal finding in toddlers"],
 "Extra right-sided volume delays P2 in every breath, so the split stays wide and fixed.","\"ASD = Always Split, Doesn't change.\""),
("Heart sounds","Which statements about S3 are correct?"+SA,["It is caused by rapid ventricular filling","It may be normal in a quiet infant with a fast heart rate","It sounds like \"Ken-TUCK-y\"","It is best heard at the apex with the bell","It is easier to hear in the left lateral decubitus position"],["It is always pathologic"],
 "S3 = early diastole, rapid filling, can be normal in children. S4 is the one that is ALWAYS pathologic.","\"S3 = Kentucky: can be a normal Kid. S4 = Tennessee: Trouble.\""),
("Heart sounds","Which statements about S4 are correct?"+SA,["It is always pathologic","It sounds like \"TEN-nessee\"","It occurs in late diastole just before S1","It is low-pitched and heard best at the apex with the bell"],["It is a normal finding in quiet infants","It is best heard with the diaphragm at the ULSB"],
 "S4 reflects forceful atrial contraction into a stiff/distended ventricle. Always pathologic.","\"S4 = Tennessee = Trouble, every time.\""),
("Heart sounds","A gallop that sounds like \"Kentucky\" occurs during which part of the cardiac cycle?",["Early diastole"],["Late diastole, just before S1","Midsystole","Early systole, immediately after S1"],
 "S3 is early diastole (right after S2). S4 is late diastole (right before S1).","\"Ken-TUCK-y: the extra beat TUCKs in right AFTER S2.\""),
# ===== Clicks
("Clicks","Which statements describe a PULMONIC ejection click?"+SA,["It occurs early in systole, right after S1","It is high in frequency","It varies with respiration","It disappears with inspiration"],["It is constant in intensity","It is heard best at the apex in midsystole"],
 "Pulmonic click varies with breathing and disappears on inspiration. The aortic click is the constant one.","\"Pulmonic click Plays with breathing. Aortic click is Always the same.\""),
("Clicks","A teen has a MIDSYSTOLIC click at the apex that is loudest when standing or leaning forward. The most likely cause is:",["Mitral valve prolapse"],["Aortic stenosis","Pulmonic stenosis","Patent ductus arteriosus"],
 "Nonejection clicks are midsystolic at the apex, best heard leaning forward or standing, and are due to MVP.","\"MVP = Middle click, apex, Very upright (standing).\""),
("Clicks","An ejection click may be confused with which normal-sounding finding?",["A split first heart sound"],["A split second heart sound","An S3 gallop","A venous hum"],
 "Ejection clicks occur right after S1 and may sound like a split S1.","\"Click right after lubb → sounds like lubb-lubb.\""),
("Clicks","Per Box 38.2, which sounds point to a POSSIBLY PATHOLOGIC murmur?"+SA,["Systolic clicks","Opening snaps","An accentuated S2","S4 gallop","Fixed splitting of S2 (not from bundle branch block)"],["S2 that splits with inspiration and becomes single with expiration"],
 "Extra sounds (clicks, snaps, S4) and abnormal S2 (fixed or loud) raise concern. Normal physiologic splitting is reassuring.","\"Extra noises = extra workup.\""),
# ===== Murmur description & grading
("Murmur basics","Which factors can make ANY murmur louder?"+SA,["Fever","Anemia","Exercise","Anxiety"],["Lying still and calm","Dehydration with low cardiac output"],
 "Anything that increases cardiac output intensifies murmurs.","\"FEAR makes murmurs louder\": Fever, Exercise, Anemia, Restlessness/anxiety."),
("Murmur basics","Why might a child with a LARGE septal defect have NO murmur?",["There may be little turbulent flow across a large, nonrestrictive opening"],["Large defects always close by 6 months","Murmurs are only heard with small hearts","The stethoscope can't pick up septal defects"],
 "Murmurs come from turbulence. Large nonrestrictive defects (or a nonrestrictive PDA) may not create turbulence.","\"No noise ≠ no problem.\""),
("Murmur basics","Up to what percentage of children have an innocent murmur at some point?",["80%"],["5%","20%","100%"],
 "Up to 80% of children have an innocent (functional) murmur from normal flow through normal structures.","\"8 out of 10 kids hum a little.\""),
("Murmur basics","Which characteristics are included when describing a heart murmur?"+SA,["Timing in the cardiac cycle","Location where loudest","Radiation","Quality","Pitch","Grade (intensity)"],["Blood type"],
 "Table 38.4: timing, location, radiation, quality, duration, pitch, grade.","\"Tell Little Rachel Quietly: Pitch, Duration, Grade\" (Timing, Location, Radiation, Quality, Pitch, Duration, Grade)."),
("Murmur basics","Which statements about murmur GRADE are correct?"+SA,["It does not necessarily indicate how severe the problem is","It may change from supine to sitting"],["A grade II murmur always means a small defect","Grade VI is the most common innocent murmur","A thrill first appears at grade II"],
 "Intensity ≠ severity; a small VSD can be loud. A thrill begins at grade IV.","\"Loud ≠ bad, soft ≠ safe.\""),
("Murmur basics","A murmur is loud, heard over a wide area, with a palpable thrill, but CANNOT be heard with the stethoscope barely touching the chest. Grade?",["IV/VI"],["III/VI","V/VI","VI/VI"],
 "IV = loud + thrill. V = heard with the stethoscope barely on the chest. VI = heard without a stethoscope.","\"4 = Feel it (thrill). 5 = barely touching. 6 = no touching.\""),
("Murmur basics","A murmur can be heard with the stethoscope held just off the chest. What grade is it?",["VI/VI"],["IV/VI","V/VI","III/VI"],
 "Grade VI is heard without the stethoscope touching the chest (rare).","\"6 = Sixth sense, no stethoscope on the chest.\""),
("Murmur timing","Which statements about DIASTOLIC murmurs are correct?"+SA,["They always indicate cardiac pathology","A decrescendo murmur starting with S2 is often aortic or pulmonic regurgitation","A mid-diastolic rumble at the apex can come from increased flow with a large VSD or PDA"],["Most innocent murmurs are diastolic","They occur between S1 and S2"],
 "Diastolic murmurs occur between S2 and S1 and are always pathologic.","\"Diastolic is always Dangerous.\""),
("Murmur timing","Which statements about SYSTOLIC murmurs are correct?"+SA,["Most murmurs heard are systolic","They include all innocent murmurs","A VSD causes a regurgitant holosystolic murmur","Aortic stenosis causes an ejection murmur"],["Systolic murmurs are always pathologic"],
 "Systolic murmurs can be innocent or pathologic; diastolic murmurs are always pathologic.","\"Systolic = Sometimes fine. Diastolic = Danger.\""),
("Murmur timing","What is the most common cause of a CONTINUOUS murmur in children?",["Patent ductus arteriosus"],["Atrial septal defect","Aortic stenosis","Still murmur"],
 "A continuous murmur runs from S1 through systole AND diastole. PDA is the most common cause; it must be told apart from a venous hum.","\"Continuous Machinery = PDA.\""),
("Murmur timing","A continuous murmur below the clavicles disappears when the child lies down or turns the head. This is most likely:",["Venous hum"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","Aortic regurgitation","Coarctation"],
 "A venous hum is innocent and continuous, but disappears with head turning, jugular compression, or lying supine. A PDA murmur cannot be suppressed.","\"Hum goes Home when you lie down.\""),
# ===== Innocent vs pathologic (Box 38.2)
("Innocent vs pathologic","Which features are typical of an INNOCENT murmur (Box 38.2)?"+SA,["Grade I–III and localized","Changes with position","Musical or vibratory quality","Short and peaking in the first half of systole","Normal vital signs and ECG"],["Associated with a palpable thrill","Pansystolic timing"],
 "Innocent murmurs are soft, short, systolic, positional, musical, and occur in an otherwise healthy child.","\"7 S's\": Soft, Systolic, Short, Single, Small area, Sweet, Sensitive (changes with position)."),
("Innocent vs pathologic","Which findings suggest a POSSIBLY PATHOLOGIC murmur?"+SA,["Any diastolic murmur","Harsh quality","Grade IV/VI or louder","A murmur that does not change with position","A murmur in a child with trisomy 21"],["Musical quality that disappears with Valsalva"],
 "Box 38.2 pathologic list includes diastolic, thrill, pansystolic, nonsuppressible continuous, clicks, snaps, fixed/accentuated S2, S4, non-positional, grade ≥ IV, harsh, or a syndrome linked to CHD.","\"Harsh, Loud, Diastolic, Doesn't move, or Down syndrome → refer.\""),
("Innocent vs pathologic","A family is told their child has an innocent murmur. Which teaching is correct?"+SA,["The murmur may come and go","It may be louder with fever, anxiety, pain, or exercise","No activity restriction is needed"],["The child needs dental antibiotic prophylaxis","The child should avoid competitive sports"],
 "Reassure: no pathology, no restrictions, no special precautions.","\"Innocent = Normal kid, Normal life.\""),
("Innocent vs pathologic","Which innocent-murmur finding may make the murmur DISAPPEAR?",["Valsalva maneuver, position change, or gentle jugular pressure"],["Exercise","Fever","Anemia"],
 "Box 38.2: innocent murmurs may disappear with Valsalva, position, or gentle jugular pressure. Fever, exercise, and anemia make them louder.","\"Innocent murmurs are shy: change position and they hide.\""),
# ===== Innocent murmur types (Table 38.5)
("Innocent murmur types","A 4-year-old has a grade II midsystolic, vibratory, \"twangy string\" murmur at the LLSB that is louder lying down and softer standing. This is:",["Still murmur"],["Venous hum","Pulmonary flow murmur of infancy","Small VSD"],
 "Still murmur: ages 2–6, LLSB/apex, musical/vibratory, louder supine, decreases standing, does not radiate.","\"Still = Strings (twangy guitar), Supine louder.\""),
("Innocent murmur types","Which conditions are in the differential diagnosis of a Still murmur?"+SA,["Small VSD","Idiopathic hypertrophic subaortic stenosis (IHSS)"],["Patent ductus arteriosus","Tetralogy of Fallot","Coarctation"],
 "Table 38.5 lists small VSD and IHSS as the differential for Still murmur (both are at the LLSB).","\"Still sits at the LLSB next to its look-alikes: VSD and IHSS.\""),
("Innocent murmur types","A preterm newborn has a soft murmur from the left upper sternal border radiating to both axillae and the back. It is expected to:",["Disappear by about 6 months of age"],["Require PDA ligation","Get louder throughout childhood","Indicate tetralogy of Fallot"],
 "Pulmonary flow murmur of infancy (peripheral pulmonary stenosis) is common in newborns, especially preterm, radiates to the axillae and back, and is usually gone by 6 months.","\"PPS = Preemie Pulmonary Swish, gone by 6 months.\""),
("Innocent murmur types","A thin-chested 12-year-old has a soft, blowing systolic murmur at the pulmonic area with a normally split S2 and no click or thrill. This is most likely:",["Pulmonary flow murmur of childhood"],["Atrial septal defect","Pulmonic stenosis","Venous hum"],
 "Pulmonary flow murmur of childhood: thin-chested kids 8–14, soft blowing, normal split S2, no click or thrill. ASD would have a FIXED split; PS would have a click.","\"Thin teen, soft blow, Normal split = Normal flow.\""),
("Innocent murmur types","Which findings describe a VENOUS HUM?"+SA,["Constant swishing sound","Heard below the clavicles in the upright position","Disappears with head turning or jugular compression","Disappears when supine"],["Heard best at the apex","Always requires cardiology referral"],
 "Venous hum = innocent continuous murmur; differential is PDA.","\"Hum = Head turn or Horizontal makes it vanish.\""),
# ===== Diagnostics
("Diagnostic studies","A primary care provider plans to refer a child to pediatric cardiology for a murmur. What does the text recommend about ordering routine tests first?",["Routine tests before referral are not cost-effective; cardiology decides which tests to order"],["Always order an ECG and chest x-ray before referral","Order an echocardiogram first, then refer","Order a CBC, ECG, and MRI first"],
 "The cardiologist can decide more precisely which tests are needed.","\"Referring? Don't pre-test, let the expert pick.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","A cyanotic newborn receives 100% oxygen and pinks up, with a rising saturation. This suggests:",["The cause is primarily pulmonary"],["The cause is primarily cardiac","The test is invalid in newborns","Tetralogy of Fallot"],
 "Hyperoxia test: pinking with 100% O2 = lung disease. Minimal or no improvement = heart disease (the blood is bypassing the lungs).","\"Pinks up = Pulmonary. Stays blue = Heart (Cardiac).\""),
("Diagnostic studies","Why might a CBC be ordered in a child with a murmur?",["To rule out severe anemia or polycythemia as a cause"],["To diagnose a VSD","To measure pulmonary vascular resistance","To detect a heart rhythm problem"],
 "Anemia increases flow (louder murmur); polycythemia can reflect chronic cyanosis.","\"CBC checks the blood, not the hole.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","What information does a chest radiograph provide in cardiac evaluation?"+SA,["Heart size and size of specific chambers","Cardiac contour","Status of pulmonary blood flow","Condition of the lungs"],["Exact location of a septal defect","Heart rhythm"],
 "CXR shows size, shape, pulmonary flow, and lungs. Echo locates defects; ECG shows rhythm.","\"X-ray = Size and Shape.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","Which can be done during cardiac catheterization?"+SA,["Measure pulmonary vascular resistance and cardiac output","Obtain a heart tissue sample","Open narrowed valves or arteries","Deploy devices to close holes or extra vessels"],["Screen for hearing loss","Diagnose CHD at 10 weeks' gestation"],
 "Cath = diagnostic AND interventional.","\"Cath can Measure, Biopsy, Open, and Plug.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","How early can fetal echocardiography diagnose congenital heart disease?",["16–18 weeks' gestation (as early as 10 weeks with high-frequency transvaginal echo)"],["Only after birth","At 32 weeks","At 4 weeks"],
 "Fetal echo detects CHD, arrhythmias, and hemodynamic changes before birth.","\"Sweet 16 for the fetal heart.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","Which ECG pattern is classic for an atrial septal defect?",["Right axis deviation with an rSR′ (right bundle branch block) pattern in V1"],["Superior axis between −40 and −160 degrees","Left ventricular hypertrophy with inverted T waves","Delta waves"],
 "ASD → right-sided volume load: RAD, RAE, RBBB rSR′ in V1. Superior axis = AV septal defect.","\"ASD loads the Right: Right axis, Right bundle.\""),
("Diagnostic studies","Which ECG finding is characteristic of an AV septal (endocardial cushion) defect?",["Superior axis (−40 to −160 degrees)"],["Right bundle branch block only","Normal axis with LVH only","Prolonged QT"],
 "AVSD shows a superior QRS axis; RVH is common and the PR is prolonged in 50%.","\"AV canal points UP (superior axis).\""),
# ===== Primary care management
("Primary care management","An infant with CHF is not gaining weight. Which nutrition strategies are appropriate?"+SA,["Formula or fortified breast milk at 24, 27, or 30 kcal/oz","Nasogastric tube feeding if the infant fatigues with feeding","Referral to a nutritionist"],["Diluting formula to reduce fluid volume","Limiting feeds to every 6 hours"],
 "Infants with CHF burn more calories and tire with feeding: increase caloric density and use an NG or G tube if needed.","\"Pack the calories, not the volume: 24-27-30.\""),
("Primary care management","An infant had cardiopulmonary bypass surgery and received blood products. When can MMR and varicella be given?",["6 months after bypass and blood/plasma exposure"],["Immediately; there is no delay","11 months after surgery","Never"],
 "Ch 38: delay live vaccines 6 months after bypass and RBC/plasma exposure. Other vaccines stay on schedule. (Compare: after 2 g/kg IVIG for Kawasaki, the wait is 11 months.)","\"Bypass → wait 6 for live. IVIG (Kawasaki) → wait 11.\""),
("Primary care management","Which infants qualify for RSV prophylaxis per the chapter?"+SA,["Infants under 1 year with cyanotic CHD","Infants under 1 year with complicated CHD and CHF","Infants under 1 year with CHD and pulmonary hypertension"],["A healthy 3-year-old with a repaired small ASD","An infant with an innocent Still murmur"],
 "AAP: RSV prophylaxis for infants younger than 1 year with cyanotic or complicated CHD, especially with CHF or pulmonary HTN.","\"Under 1, Blue or Burdened (CHF/pulm HTN) → RSV shield.\""),
("Primary care management","What is the RSV prophylaxis regimen described in the chapter?",["15 mg/kg IM every 30 days during RSV season, maximum 5 doses"],["15 mg/kg IV once","50 mg/kg PO daily","5 mg/kg IM weekly, maximum 15 doses"],
 "Maximum 5 doses of 15 mg/kg IM monthly during the season.","\"15 every 30, up to 5.\""),
("Primary care management","A 45-year-old grandmother will babysit an infant with CHD. Her last Td was 3 years ago. What should she receive?",["Tdap now, regardless of the interval since her last Td"],["Nothing; Td is still valid","Td booster in 7 years","MMR only"],
 "Anyone older than 19 spending time with infants should get Tdap, regardless of the Td interval, to prevent pertussis.","\"Cocoon the baby: Tdap for every adult around them.\""),
("Primary care management","Which measures help prevent avoidable complications in children with CHD?"+SA,["Good handwashing","Avoiding contact with people who have URI symptoms","Seasonal influenza vaccine for the infant and caregivers","Excellent dental hygiene"],["Withholding all vaccines until after surgery","Restricting all physical activity"],
 "Preventing respiratory infections and keeping gums healthy (to limit endocarditis) are key.","\"Hands, Flu shot, Floss.\""),
("Primary care management","Which statements about physical activity for children with heart disease are correct?"+SA,["Most children self-limit their activity","If the child can talk comfortably during activity, the intensity is likely appropriate","Children with ventricular arrhythmias may need complete activity restriction","Cardiology should be consulted before strenuous sports"],["All children with CHD must avoid all sports"],
 "Encourage daily activity. The \"talk test\" guides intensity. Specific conditions need specific limits.","\"Talk test: if you can talk, you can walk (and play).\""),
("Primary care management","Infant and child CPR certification is recommended for:",["Anyone caring for a child with a heart condition"],["Only the cardiologist","Only parents of children with TOF","Only school nurses"],
 "The chapter says CPR certification is critical for anyone caring for a child with a heart condition.","\"Heart kid at home? CPR for everyone.\""),
# ===== IE prophylaxis
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","Which conditions qualify for dental endocarditis prophylaxis (Box 38.3)?"+SA,["Prosthetic heart valve","Previous infective endocarditis","Unrepaired cyanotic CHD, including palliative shunts","CHD repaired with prosthetic material, within 6 months of repair","Heart transplant recipient with valve disease"],["Small isolated VSD that was never repaired"],
 "Only the highest-risk conditions qualify. A small VSD does not require SBE prophylaxis.","\"Plastic, Past IE, Purple (cyanotic), Patch <6 months, Post-transplant valve.\""),
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","A child had a residual VSD next to a surgical patch. Prophylaxis is needed:",["Indefinitely, because there is a residual defect at or adjacent to the prosthetic patch"],["Only for 6 months after surgery","Never","Only before tonsillectomy"],
 "Repaired CHD with residual defects at or near a prosthetic patch or device stays high-risk.","\"Leftover leak by the patch = lifelong cover.\""),
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","What is the oral dental prophylaxis regimen for a child without a penicillin allergy?",["Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg as a single dose 30–60 minutes before the procedure"],["Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg daily for 10 days","Amoxicillin 90 mg/kg/day divided BID","Clindamycin 20 mg/kg after the procedure"],
 "Single dose, 30–60 minutes BEFORE the procedure.","\"50 mg/kg, 1 dose, 30–60 minutes before.\""),
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","A child who qualifies for prophylaxis had hives (urticaria) with amoxicillin and can take oral medications. Which choices are appropriate?"+SA,["Clindamycin 20 mg/kg","Azithromycin 15 mg/kg"],["Cephalexin 50 mg/kg","Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg","Ampicillin 50 mg/kg IM"],
 "Cephalosporins should NOT be used with a history of anaphylaxis, angioedema, or urticaria to penicillins.","\"Hives to Pen? Skip the Cephs: Clinda or Azithro.\""),
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","A child needing dental prophylaxis cannot take oral medication and has no penicillin allergy. Which are acceptable?"+SA,["Ampicillin 50 mg/kg IM or IV","Cefazolin or ceftriaxone 50 mg/kg IM or IV"],["Clindamycin 20 mg/kg PO","Amoxicillin 50 mg/kg PO","Azithromycin 15 mg/kg PO"],
 "Non-oral options without allergy: ampicillin, cefazolin, or ceftriaxone.","\"No mouth? Amp or a Ceph in the muscle or vein.\""),
("Endocarditis prophylaxis","After endocarditis prophylaxis is given for a procedure, which findings should raise suspicion for IE?"+SA,["New fever","Petechiae"],["Improved appetite","Normal heart rate"],
 "Maintain a high index of suspicion for IE if fever or petechiae appear after any procedure.","\"Fever + spots after a procedure = think IE.\""),
# ===== Referral / genetics / teratogens
("Referral","Which findings should prompt referral to pediatric cardiology?"+SA,["Oxygen saturation less than 95%","Symptoms of CHF","A pathologic murmur","A hard-to-classify murmur in a child with poor growth"],["A Still murmur in a thriving 4-year-old"],
 "These are the chapter's findings suggestive of cardiac disease.","\"Under 95, Failing, Funny murmur, or Failing to grow.\""),
("Referral","An otherwise well 6-year-old has a new possibly pathologic murmur. How quickly should they be referred?",["Timely but not urgent"],["Immediately to the emergency department","Only if symptoms develop","Within 1 hour"],
 "A child with a murmur who is otherwise doing well is referred in a timely but not urgent manner.","\"Well kid + murmur = soon, not siren.\""),
("Referral","Which presentations require referral AS SOON AS POSSIBLE?"+SA,["A teen with chest pain during exertion","A child with syncope","A newborn with cyanosis and poor feeding","A child with an arrhythmia and dizziness"],["A well child whose innocent murmur gets louder with fever"],
 "Exertional chest pain, syncope, arrhythmia, dyspnea, CHF signs, abnormal vital signs, or a symptomatic newborn → ASAP.","\"Pain with play, Passing out, Palpitations, Purple newborn = Prompt.\""),
("Genetics and causes","Which situations warrant a genetic testing referral for a child with CHD?"+SA,["Other congenital anomalies","Dysmorphic features","Neurocognitive deficits","Growth retardation","Mother with multiple miscarriages"],["Child with an innocent murmur and normal development"],
 "About 35% of CHD is linked to genetic factors.","\"Heart + something else = Gene check.\""),
("Genetics and causes","During which weeks of gestation does CHD develop?",["2nd to 8th weeks"],["12th to 16th weeks","20th to 24th weeks","Last trimester"],
 "This is why exposures early in pregnancy (often before a woman knows she is pregnant) matter.","\"The heart is built in weeks 2–8.\""),
("Genetics and causes","Which maternal exposures are linked to CHD?"+SA,["Lithium","Retinoic acid","Ibuprofen or naproxen","ACE inhibitors","Alcohol"],["Acetaminophen in normal doses"],
 "Teratogens listed: lithium, retinoic acid, antiepileptics, NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, TCAs, sulfonamides, sulfasalazine, tobacco, alcohol, cocaine, marijuana.","\"LIRA ACT: Lithium, Isotretinoin (retinoic acid), NSAIDs (ibuprofen), ACE inhibitors, Alcohol, Cocaine/cannabis, Tobacco.\""),
("Genetics and causes","Which maternal conditions are associated with CHD?"+SA,["Diabetes mellitus","Phenylketonuria (PKU)","Rubella","Febrile illness such as influenza","Connective tissue disorders"],["Seasonal allergies"],
 "Maternal illnesses linked to CHD: diabetes, connective tissue disease, PKU, rubella, febrile illness (especially flu).","\"Mom's DRiP-F: Diabetes, Rubella, PKU, Fever (flu).\""),
("Genetics and causes","A mother asks whether something she did caused her baby's heart defect. Which response best reflects the chapter?",["\"Most heart defects have no identifiable cause.\""],["\"It was caused by stress during pregnancy.\"","\"It was definitely genetic from your side.\"","\"It happened during labor.\""],
 "Parents often wrongly blame themselves; most CHD has no identifiable cause. Clear information reduces needless guilt.","\"Not your fault: most have no known cause.\""),
# ===== Syndromes
("Syndromes","Which heart defects are associated with trisomy 21 (Down syndrome)?"+SA,["AV septal defect","VSD","ASD","PDA","Tetralogy of Fallot"],["Supravalvular aortic stenosis"],
 "About 50% of children with trisomy 21 have CHD; complete AV canal is classic. Supravalvular AS = Williams.","\"Down = AV canal first, then the D's and TOF.\""),
("Syndromes","A girl with Turner syndrome (monosomy X) is most at risk for:",["Bicuspid aortic valve and coarctation of the aorta"],["Supravalvular aortic stenosis","AV septal defect","Dextrocardia"],
 "Turner: bicuspid AV, CoA (35%), pulmonic stenosis. Also check four-extremity BPs.","\"Turner Turns the aorta: Coarctation and Bicuspid valve.\""),
("Syndromes","Which heart defects are associated with 22q11.2 deletion (DiGeorge syndrome)?"+SA,["Interrupted aortic arch","Truncus arteriosus","Tetralogy of Fallot","Perimembranous VSD"],["Supravalvular aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse"],
 "DiGeorge = conotruncal (outflow) defects.","\"CATCH-22: Cardiac outflow (TOF, Truncus, Interrupted arch).\""),
("Syndromes","Williams syndrome (7q11.23 deletion) is classically associated with:",["Supravalvular aortic stenosis and pulmonic stenosis"],["Coarctation and bicuspid aortic valve","AV septal defect","Transposition of the great arteries"],
 "Williams = supravalvular AS and pulmonic stenosis.","\"Williams sings ABOVE the valve\" (supravalvular)."),
("Syndromes","Noonan syndrome is associated with which cardiac problems?"+SA,["Valvular pulmonic stenosis","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],["Aortic root dilation","Tricuspid atresia","Truncus arteriosus"],
 "Noonan (PTPN11): valvular PS and HCM. Short stature is common.","\"Noonan = PS + HCM: Pulmonic and thick Heart.\""),
("Syndromes","An infant of a diabetic mother has the highest listed risk of which cardiac problems?"+SA,["Transposition of the great arteries","VSD","Coarctation","Cardiomyopathy"],["Supravalvular aortic stenosis","Mitral valve prolapse from Marfan"],
 "Infant of diabetic mother: TGA, VSD, CoA (3–5%); cardiomyopathy (10–20%).","\"Sugar Mom → Switched arteries (TGA) and a thick heart.\""),
("Syndromes","Which pairing of syndrome and heart defect is correct?",["Holt-Oram syndrome: ASD and VSD"],["Marfan syndrome: tricuspid atresia","Williams syndrome: AV canal","Trisomy 13: aortic root dilation"],
 "Holt-Oram (TBX5) = ASD/VSD (with upper-limb anomalies). Marfan = MVP and aortic root dilation. Trisomy 13 = VSD, PDA, dextrocardia.","\"Holt-Oram: Hands + Holes.\""),
# ===== CHF
("Heart failure","Which are signs of CHF in INFANTS (Box 38.4)?"+SA,["Tachypnea","Diaphoresis","Periorbital edema","Hepatomegaly","Poor feeding and poor weight gain"],["Orthopnea"],
 "Orthopnea and peripheral edema are listed for children and teens, not infants. Infants show periorbital edema.","\"Infant HF = Sweaty, Speedy, Swollen eyes, Stuck weight.\""),
("Heart failure","Which CHF signs are listed for children and teens but NOT infants?"+SA,["Orthopnea","Dyspnea with exertion","Peripheral edema"],["Tachypnea","Tachycardia"],
 "Older kids show adult-like signs: orthopnea, exertional dyspnea, peripheral edema.","\"Older kids get the grown-up signs.\""),
("Heart failure","What is the LARGEST group of children with CHF?",["Those with excessive left-to-right shunting through unrepaired defects"],["Those with myocarditis","Those with rheumatic fever","Those with Kawasaki disease"],
 "L→R shunts (VSD, PDA, AV canal) cause most pediatric CHF. The myocardium often copes, but volume overload causes symptoms.","\"Too much to the lungs = most HF in kids.\""),
("Heart failure","Which are traditional pediatric heart failure therapies?"+SA,["Diuretics","Inotropes","Afterload reducers"],["Prostaglandin E1 for every child","Fluid boluses"],
 "Diuretics, inotropes, and afterload reducers (e.g., ACE inhibitors). β-blockers and aldosterone inhibitors target neurohormones.","\"Dump, Pump, Unload.\""),
("Heart failure","Which statement about B-type natriuretic peptide (BNP) monitoring is correct?",["It may help in biventricular heart failure but is not recommended in single-ventricle disease"],["It is required for every infant with a murmur","It diagnoses VSD","It is most useful after a Fontan"],
 "BNP is secreted by stretched ventricles. It's useful in biventricular HF, not single-ventricle disease.","\"BNP: Both ventricles, yes. One ventricle, no.\""),
("Heart failure","Which lesions typically cause CHF in the FIRST WEEK of life?"+SA,["Hypoplastic left heart syndrome","Critical aortic stenosis","Coarctation of the aorta"],["Small VSD","Rheumatic fever"],
 "Duct-dependent left-sided obstructions present early as the ductus closes. VSD-related CHF appears later (weeks), after PVR falls.","\"Week 1 = Left-side blockages crash as the duct closes.\""),
# ===== ASD
("ASD","Which type of ASD is most common?",["Ostium secundum (mid-septum near the foramen ovale)"],["Ostium primum","Sinus venosus","Unroofed coronary sinus"],
 "Secundum is most common. Sinus venosus is often linked with anomalous pulmonary venous return; primum is seen in Down syndrome; unroofed coronary sinus is rarest.","\"Secundum = Second door in the Middle (foramen ovale).\""),
("ASD","In a child with an ASD, the systolic murmur is caused by:",["Increased flow across the pulmonary valve"],["Blood flowing through the atrial hole","Mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],
 "The grade I–III crescendo-decrescendo murmur at the pulmonic area comes from extra flow across the pulmonary valve, not from the hole itself.","\"The hole is silent; the valve sings.\""),
("ASD","Which findings may be found in a child with an ASD?"+SA,["Frequent respiratory infections or pneumonia","Thin build or exertional dyspnea","Wide, relatively fixed split S2","Murmur first noticed at 2–3 years of age"],["Severe cyanosis at birth"],
 "Many are asymptomatic; symptoms may increase in late adolescence or adulthood.","\"ASD = A Silent Defect that splits S2.\""),
("ASD","Which statements about ASD management are correct?"+SA,["Small defects found in infancy may close on their own","Device closure in the cath lab is possible if the child weighs more than 15 kg","SBE prophylaxis is needed only for 6 months after closure","Low-dose aspirin is given for 6 months after device closure","Exercise restriction is unnecessary"],["Lifelong SBE prophylaxis is required"],
 "Large defects are closed after age 1, ideally before school entry.","\"ASD device: 15 kg, 6 months aspirin, 6 months prophylaxis.\""),
("ASD","Which are complications of an UNTREATED ASD?"+SA,["Right ventricular enlargement and failure","Paradoxical emboli","Pulmonary hypertension"],["Coronary artery aneurysm","Left ventricular outflow obstruction"],
 "A clot can cross the hole into the systemic circulation (paradoxical embolus).","\"Right side wears out, clots sneak across.\""),
# ===== VSD
("VSD","Which type of VSD is most common?",["Perimembranous"],["Muscular","Inlet","Supracristal"],
 "Four types: perimembranous (most common), supracristal, inlet, muscular.","\"Peri = Popular.\""),
("VSD","An infant's VSD murmur becomes HIGHER-PITCHED over several months. This suggests:",["The defect is getting smaller"],["The defect is getting larger","Pulmonary hypertension is developing","Endocarditis"],
 "Smaller holes create higher-pitched, harsher jets.","\"Higher pitch = Hole shrinking.\""),
("VSD","Which findings are expected with a LARGE VSD?"+SA,["Low-pitched holosystolic murmur at the LLSB","Diastolic rumble at the apex","Thrill along the left sternal border","Signs of CHF after the first weeks of life","S3 or S4 gallop if CHF is present"],["Grade I soft musical murmur that disappears standing"],
 "Large VSD = lots of flow (rumble at the apex from increased mitral flow) and CHF as PVR falls.","\"Big VSD: Low pitch, Rumble, Thrill, Failure.\""),
("VSD","An infant has a small VSD and no CHF symptoms. Which plan is correct?"+SA,["Monitor every 6 months during the first year","SBE prophylaxis is not recommended","Expect that many small defects close by about age 4"],["Immediate surgical closure","Lifelong activity restriction"],
 "30–50% of VSDs are small and most close spontaneously by 4 years.","\"Small VSD: Watch, no pills, often seals by 4.\""),
("VSD","An infant with a pinhole VSD has a murmur, but the echocardiogram does not show the defect. The nurse understands:",["Pinhole VSDs may cause a murmur yet not be visualized on echo"],["The murmur must be innocent","The echo must be repeated immediately","Cardiac catheterization is required"],
 "The chapter notes that in \"pinhole\" VSDs a murmur may be present while the defect isn't seen on echo.","\"Pinhole: heard, not seen.\""),
("VSD","A transcatheter device can be used to close a muscular VSD only if the child weighs more than:",["6 kg"],["15 kg","3 kg","25 kg"],
 "VSD device > 6 kg. Compare: ASD device > 15 kg.","\"VSD 6, ASD 15.\""),
# ===== AVSD
("AV septal defect","A COMPLETE AV septal defect includes which components?"+SA,["Primum ASD","Single (common) AV valve","Inlet VSD"],["Overriding aorta","Pulmonary stenosis"],
 "Absence of the endocardial cushion leads to a primum ASD, a common AV valve, and an inlet VSD.","\"Complete canal = Atrial hole + Ventricle hole + One valve.\""),
("AV septal defect","An infant with trisomy 21 and a complete AV canal shows NO CHF signs but has a loud single S2, a precordial heave, minimal murmur, and desaturation with crying. This suggests:",["Persistent high pulmonary vascular resistance (pulmonary hypertension)"],["The defect has closed","An innocent murmur","Mild disease needing no follow-up"],
 "Some infants with trisomy 21 keep high neonatal PVR, so they don't overcirculate (no CHF) but develop pulmonary HTN. A quiet murmur here is NOT reassuring.","\"Down + quiet murmur + loud S2 = Pressure, not peace.\""),
("AV septal defect","When is surgical correction of a COMPLETE AV canal defect usually done?",["Before 6 months of age"],["At school entry","After age 5","Only if symptoms appear in adolescence"],
 "Early repair prevents irreversible pulmonary vascular disease. Partial AV canal is repaired in toddler/preschool years.","\"Complete canal: fix before 6 months.\""),
# ===== PDA
("PDA","In a term infant, when does the ductus arteriosus normally close?",["Functionally in 12–72 hours; permanently in 2–3 weeks"],["Functionally at 6 months","Permanently within 1 hour","Only after surgery"],
 "Functional closure 12–72 h, permanent closure 2–3 weeks (term).","\"Ductus: 3 days to shut, 3 weeks to seal.\""),
("PDA","Which infant is at HIGHEST risk for a PDA?",["A premature infant weighing less than 1750 g"],["A term male infant","A post-term infant","An infant of an older father"],
 "PDA frequency rises with prematurity (45–80% under 1750 g). Female:male ratio is 3:1.","\"Tiny preemie = Patent ductus.\""),
("PDA","Indomethacin is ordered for a 3-month-old TERM infant with a PDA. The nurse should question it because:",["Indomethacin is contraindicated and ineffective in term or older infants"],["The dose is too low","It must be given orally","It only works in girls"],
 "Indomethacin/ibuprofen closure works only in preterm infants.","\"Indomethacin is for Infants born early only.\""),
("PDA","Which statements about PDA findings and management are correct?"+SA,["After the first weeks, a continuous machinery murmur is heard in the left infraclavicular area","Coils or plugs can close many PDAs in the cath lab in children older than 8 months","Surgical ligation does not require cardiopulmonary bypass","SBE prophylaxis is recommended for 6 months after device closure"],["The murmur is continuous immediately after birth","Families should expect lifelong activity limits"],
 "Right after birth, the murmur is soft and systolic. It becomes continuous after the first weeks.","\"PDA: early whisper (systolic), later Machinery.\""),
# ===== Cyanotic general & TOF
("Cyanotic CHD","At approximately what oxygen saturation does cyanosis become VISIBLE?",["85%"],["95%","75%","65%"],
 "Visible cyanosis appears at about 85%. It is easier to see with polycythemia and harder with anemia or fetal hemoglobin.","\"85 = you start to see blue.\""),
("Cyanotic CHD","Cyanosis is LESS readily apparent in which situations?"+SA,["Anemia","Presence of fetal hemoglobin"],["Polycythemia","Cold environment only","Right-to-left shunting"],
 "Less hemoglobin (anemia) or high-affinity fetal Hb hides cyanosis. Polycythemia makes it more obvious.","\"Anemia and Newborn blood Hide the blue.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which statements about Tetralogy of Fallot are correct?"+SA,["It is the most common cyanotic heart lesion","Cyanosis usually increases over the first months as RVOT obstruction worsens","\"Pink tets\" may have little cyanosis","It is more common with 22q11.2 deletion and Down syndrome"],["It is the most common acyanotic lesion","It always presents with severe cyanosis at birth"],
 "TOF has a spectrum of severity. Mild cases act like a large VSD, and cyanosis is usually present by 6 months.","\"TOF: Tops the cyanotic list, Turns bluer over time.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Hypercyanotic (\"tet\") spells increase at what age and with what triggers?"+SA,["2–4 months of age","Crying","Feeding","Defecation"],["Sleeping quietly","Lying supine at rest"],
 "Anything that increases oxygen demand or lowers systemic resistance can trigger a spell.","\"Cry, Eat, Poop → Spell.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which interventions are appropriate for a hypercyanotic spell?"+SA,["Knee-chest position","Soothing the infant","Oxygen","Subcutaneous morphine as ordered"],["Laying the infant flat and supine","Giving a large feeding"],
 "Knee-chest raises systemic resistance, reducing right-to-left shunting and increasing pulmonary blood flow.","\"Tuck, Calm, O2, Morphine (TCOM).\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","Which test findings are expected in TOF?"+SA,["Boot-shaped heart with decreased pulmonary vascular markings","Right ventricular hypertrophy with right axis deviation on ECG","Rising hemoglobin and hematocrit over time"],["Egg-shaped heart","Rib notching"],
 "Egg on a string = TGA. Rib notching = coarctation.","\"Boot = TOF, Egg = TGA, Notches = Coarct.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","After TOF repair, which long-term issues need lifelong cardiology follow-up?"+SA,["Pulmonary valve regurgitation","Atrial and ventricular arrhythmias","A widening QRS duration, which raises the risk of VT and sudden death"],["Rheumatic fever","Coronary artery aneurysm"],
 "Progressive RV dilation widens the QRS; a QRS ≥ 180 ms raises the risk of VT and sudden death. Cardiology must clear for sports.","\"Fixed TOF still needs: Valve, Rhythm, QRS checks.\""),
("Tetralogy of Fallot","A child with polycythemia from cyanotic heart disease is at increased risk for:",["Cerebral thrombosis (stroke)"],["Bleeding into the joints","Hypoglycemia","Anemia"],
 "Polycythemia boosts oxygen-carrying capacity but thickens the blood. Prevent dehydration.","\"Thick blood → brain clot.\""),
# ===== TGA
("Transposition","Which findings are typical of d-transposition of the great arteries?"+SA,["Cyanosis within the first hour to first day of life","Loud, single S2","Often large for gestational age","Egg-shaped heart on x-ray"],["Boot-shaped heart","Bounding femoral pulses with wide pulse pressure"],
 "d-TGA: aorta from the RV, PA from the LV; parallel circulations. Male:female 3:1.","\"TGA = Turns blue Gigantic baby, Egg heart.\""),
("Transposition","Which interventions are expected for a newborn with d-TGA?"+SA,["IV prostaglandin E1","Balloon atrial septostomy","Arterial switch (Jatene) procedure in the first days of life","Lifelong SBE prophylaxis"],["Indomethacin to close the ductus"],
 "Keep mixing (PGE1, septostomy), then switch the arteries. Annual echo follow-up.","\"Open, Mix, Switch.\""),
("Transposition","Without treatment, what is the mortality of d-TGA?",["50% in the first month and 90% by the first year"],["10% by the first year","Nearly 0% with oxygen alone","100% within the first hour"],
 "Untreated d-TGA is usually fatal. With surgery, long-term results are excellent.","\"TGA untreated: half by 1 month, nearly all by 1 year.\""),
# ===== Single ventricle
("Single ventricle","A child with hypoplastic left heart syndrome is undergoing staged palliation. Match the procedure to its timing:",["Bidirectional SVC-to-PA anastomosis at 4–6 months; Fontan (IVC-to-PA) at 2–4 years"],["Fontan at birth; Glenn at 10 years","Arterial switch at 4–6 months","Both stages within the first week"],
 "Stage 1 (newborn shunt) → Glenn (SVC→PA) at 4–6 months → Fontan (IVC→PA) at 2–4 years. Transplant if staging fails.","\"Superior first, Inferior last: S before I, like the alphabet.\""),
("Single ventricle","Which are long-term complications of single-ventricle palliation?"+SA,["Protein-losing enteropathy","Arrhythmias","Thromboembolic events, including stroke","Decreased exercise tolerance"],["Rheumatic heart disease"],
 "Fontan physiology strains many systems. Families need lifelong support and developmental screening.","\"Fontan Four: Protein loss, Palpitations, Plugs (clots), Poor stamina.\""),
("Single ventricle","In tricuspid atresia, which statements are correct?"+SA,["Blood must cross an ASD to the left atrium","The right ventricle is small","About half of patients also have transposition of the great arteries"],["The tricuspid valve is widely open","It is acyanotic"],
 "No RA→RV path. Blood goes across the atrial septum, mixes, and the child is cyanotic.","\"Locked tricuspid → Side door (ASD) → Mixed blue blood.\""),
("Single ventricle","When is SBE prophylaxis recommended for a child with a single-ventricle defect?",["While the child remains cyanotic"],["Never","Only for 6 months after the Fontan","Only before tonsillectomy"],
 "Unrepaired or palliated cyanotic CHD (including shunts) qualifies for prophylaxis.","\"Blue = Bacterial cover.\""),
# ===== AS
("Aortic stenosis","Which statements about aortic stenosis are correct?"+SA,["Valvular stenosis is the most common type","The stenotic valve is usually bicuspid","It causes left ventricular hypertrophy","It is about 4 times more common in males","It can cause ischemia and fatal ventricular arrhythmias"],["It mainly causes right ventricular hypertrophy"],
 "Increased LV pressure load → LVH → oxygen demand outstrips coronary supply → ischemia and arrhythmias.","\"AS: Bicuspid, Boys, Big Left ventricle.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","Which physical findings suggest aortic stenosis?"+SA,["Narrow pulse pressure","Harsh grade III–IV/VI crescendo-decrescendo systolic murmur at the URSB","Thrill at the suprasternal notch","Faint early systolic click"],["Wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses"],
 "Wide pulse pressure with bounding pulses = PDA (or aortic insufficiency). AS narrows the pulse pressure.","\"AS = Narrow pulse, Neck radiation, Notch thrill.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","A child with aortic INSUFFICIENCY (regurgitation) would have which murmur?",["Early diastolic blowing murmur from the LLSB to the apex"],["Continuous machinery murmur","Midsystolic click at the apex","Holosystolic murmur at the ULSB"],
 "Aortic regurgitation = early diastolic, decrescendo, blowing. Remember: diastolic = always pathologic.","\"Leaky aortic valve = Diastolic blow.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","Which sports guidance for aortic stenosis is correct?"+SA,["Mild AS: all sports, with annual cardiac exams","Moderate AS: low-intensity sports such as golf or bowling, per cardiology","Severe AS: avoid competitive or intensive sports"],["Severe AS: all sports are fine if there are no symptoms","Mild AS: no sports allowed"],
 "Severe AS (or moderate with symptoms) risks sudden death from ventricular arrhythmias.","\"Mild = all, Moderate = mellow (golf, bowling), Severe = sit out.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","A teen had mechanical aortic valve replacement. Which teaching is essential?",["Lifelong anticoagulation with warfarin"],["No medications are needed","Aspirin for 6 months only","Avoid all vaccines"],
 "Mechanical valves are prothrombotic and require warfarin. Tissue valves have limited durability in children.","\"Metal valve = Warfarin forever.\""),
("Aortic stenosis","Which tests detect ventricular arrhythmias in a child with aortic stenosis?"+SA,["24-hour Holter monitor","30-day event monitor"],["Hyperoxia test","Tympanometry"],
 "Echo is the diagnostic test of choice for AS; Holter or event monitors catch arrhythmias.","\"Holter for the day, Event for the month.\""),
# ===== PS
("Pulmonic stenosis","Which statements about pulmonic stenosis are correct?"+SA,["Most children are asymptomatic with a murmur found on routine exam","A systolic ejection click may decrease with inspiration","Critical PS in a newborn can cause cyanosis via right-to-left flow across the foramen ovale","Short stature is common when PS occurs with Noonan or Turner syndrome"],["The click gets louder with inspiration"],
 "The pulmonic click decreases with inspiration and increases with expiration.","\"Pulmonic click quiets on the inhale.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","Balloon valvuloplasty for pulmonic stenosis is indicated when the gradient is greater than:",["50 mm Hg"],["10 mm Hg","20 mm Hg","100 mm Hg"],
 "Balloon valvuloplasty for gradients > 50 mm Hg; surgery if unsuccessful.","\"PS: over 50 → Balloon.\""),
("Pulmonic stenosis","A child with MILD pulmonic stenosis wants to play sports. Which guidance is correct?",["Treat the child normally without activity limits, but monitor for progression during growth spurts"],["Restrict all physical activity","Give lifelong SBE prophylaxis","Repair immediately"],
 "Moderate PS can progress during rapid growth (infancy, adolescence). SBE prophylaxis is not needed unless post-op (6 months) or there is prosthetic material.","\"Mild PS: Play on, but recheck in growth spurts.\""),
# ===== COA
("Coarctation","Which findings are consistent with coarctation of the aorta?"+SA,["Upper-extremity hypertension","Weak or delayed femoral pulses","Bounding brachial, radial, and carotid pulses","Rib notching on chest x-ray","Leg pain or headaches with exercise"],["Cyanosis of the lips at rest"],
 "Hypertension above the narrowing, hypotension below it. Collateral vessels notch the ribs.","\"Bounding up, Barely down, Bumpy ribs.\""),
("Coarctation","A 6-week-old with severe coarctation would most likely show:"+SA,["Tachypnea","Poor feeding","Cool lower extremities","Lower-body mottling or pallor"],["Hypertension in the legs","Bounding femoral pulses"],
 "Severe infant CoA shows up in the first 6 weeks with heart failure and poor lower-body perfusion.","\"Cold legs + poor feeding baby → check femorals.\""),
("Coarctation","Which statements about coarctation management are correct?"+SA,["PGE1 maintains or reopens the ductus in critical neonatal coarctation","Restenosis is more likely if repair occurs before 1 year of age","Blood pressure must be monitored lifelong for recoarctation","Most children with CoA have a bicuspid aortic valve"],["Lifelong SBE prophylaxis is always required"],
 "SBE prophylaxis is needed only for 6 months post-op or if prosthetic material is used.","\"Coarct: Fix it, then Follow BP forever.\""),
("Coarctation","After coarctation repair, a teen may join competitive sports if:",["The arm-leg BP gradient is less than 20 mm Hg and peak systolic BP is normal at rest and with exercise"],["At least 1 month has passed","The arm-leg gradient is less than 50 mm Hg","The child has no headaches"],
 "Also avoid high-intensity static exercise (weight lifting, wrestling) for the first year after surgery.","\"Under 20 = Good to go; no heavy lifting year 1.\""),
("Coarctation","Which ECG change develops in an OLDER child with coarctation?",["Left ventricular hypertrophy"],["Right ventricular hypertrophy only","Superior axis","Delta waves"],
 "Infants may show RVH; older children develop LVH from hypertension.","\"Older coarct = Left side bulks up.\""),
# ===== SCD
("Sudden cardiac death","What is the MOST frequent cause of exercise-related sudden cardiac death in the young?",["Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy"],["Kawasaki disease","Atrial septal defect","Innocent murmur"],
 "Cardiomyopathies, especially HCM and to a lesser extent arrhythmogenic right ventricular cardiomyopathy (ARVC), are the most common causes.","\"HCM = Heart Collapses Midgame.\""),
("Sudden cardiac death","Which ELECTRICAL abnormalities can cause sudden cardiac death?"+SA,["Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome","Congenital long QT syndrome","Brugada syndrome"],["Atrial septal defect","Still murmur"],
 "Congenital electrical abnormalities: WPW, long QT, Brugada.","\"Electric WLB: WPW, Long QT, Brugada.\""),
("Sudden cardiac death","A 13-year-old catcher collapses after a baseball hits his chest. The mechanism is:",["Commotio cordis: a blow during the T wave triggers ventricular fibrillation"],["Aortic dissection from Marfan syndrome","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Rheumatic heart disease"],
 "Blunt, nonpenetrating trauma to the midchest at the wrong moment (T wave) → VF. Immediate CPR and AED.","\"Commotio = Chest hit at the T wave.\""),
("Sudden cardiac death","Which history findings suggest HCM or ARVC in an athlete?"+SA,["Syncope","Family history of cardiomyopathy","Palpitations","Chest pain or dyspnea with exertion"],["A soft murmur that disappears when standing"],
 "HCM may have a dynamic late systolic ejection murmur (louder with standing/Valsalva) and a forceful apical beat. ARVC is more common in males around age 15.","\"Faint, Family, Flutters, Fatigue on effort = screen the heart.\""),
("Sudden cardiac death","Which ACQUIRED causes of sudden cardiac death are listed in the chapter?"+SA,["Myocarditis","Performance-enhancing drugs","Premature coronary disease from familial hypercholesterolemia","Commotio cordis"],["Wolff-Parkinson-White syndrome"],
 "WPW is congenital and electrical; the others are acquired.","\"Acquired = Germs, Drugs, Fat, Blows.\""),
]
